@database "weather"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: weather.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Weather"
@{h1}Weather

Weather gives an agent one tool that looks up the current weather and a
forecast of up to seven days for a place. It asks free public services --
met.no (the Norwegian Meteorological Institute) and wttr.in worldwide,
the US National Weather Service for places in the US -- so it needs no
account and no key. The answer starts with a few lines of plain text that
sum it up; the data follows, one entry per day. It has no hooks and no
panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The forecast tool  " link forecast}
  @{"  The sources  " link sources}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node forecast "The forecast tool"
@{h1}The forecast tool

The tool is named after the instance: @{tt}weather_forecast@{utt} in the
default setup. It looks up the place, fetches its weather from one source
and answers with a summary and the source's data.

@{h2}Parameters

The framework does not check the arguments against the schema; what
follows is what the code does with them.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
location | the place; required. What a source accepts differs, see @{" The sources " link sources}
source | met.no (default), wttr.in or weather.gov; case does not matter. Anything else answers an error
days | forecast days, counted from today; default 3, also when null. A value below 1 is taken as 1, above 7 as 7, a fraction is cut (2.9 is 2). Not a number: an error
units | metric (default) or imperial. Only wttr.in follows it; the other sources answer in their own units
summary_format | daily (default) or hourly. hourly adds each day's time steps to the met.no and wttr.in data; the summary is the same. Any other value counts as daily
@{body}

An empty or null @{tt}source@{utt}, @{tt}units@{utt} or
@{tt}summary_format@{utt} takes the default.

@{h2}Which source answers

wttr.in with more than 3 days: met.no answers instead (wttr.in has
only three).

The @{tt}source@{utt} field of the answer says which one it was.

@{h2}The answer

@{table}
Field | Meaning
summary | a few lines of text, see below
status | success or error
location | the location as it was asked for
source | the source that answered
units | metric or imperial: the units the numbers are in, which need not be the requested ones
coordinates | lat and lon the place was found at (not with wttr.in)
current | the current conditions (met.no and wttr.in; missing with weather.gov)
forecast | one entry per day; what an entry holds depends on the source
@{body}

The summary, for met.no:

@{code text}
Weather for Munich:
Currently: 12.2°C, humidity 84.4%, wind 1.5 m/s

Forecast:
  2026-09-30: 10.8°C to 24.2°C
  2026-10-01: 12.0°C to 23.6°C
  2026-10-02: 14.6°C to 16.2°C, rain expected (7.4 mm)
@{body}

@{bullet}It lists at most the first three forecast days, however many
were asked for; the data has all of them.
@{bullet}The units are those of the answer: °C for metric, °F for
imperial; wind in m/s for met.no, km/h or mph for wttr.in.
@{bullet}Humidity or wind of 0 are left out of the current line.
@{bullet}Rain is only noted for met.no: the day's total, see
@{" The sources " link sources}.
@{bullet}For weather.gov a day's range is its night and day temperature.
A day whose lowest and highest value are the same -- one weather.gov
period, a met.no first day of a single hour -- shows one value
("2026-09-29: 60°F").

@{h2}Errors

@{table}
error | When
Missing required parameter: location | location is empty or missing
days must be a whole number from 1 to 7, got 'many' | days is not a number
Unsupported weather source: <name> | an unknown source
Weather request cancelled by user | the agent's run was stopped before the lookup began; the answer also has @{tt}cancelled: true@{utt}
@{body}

A failed lookup answers @{tt}status: error@{utt} with the failure in
@{tt}error@{utt}, the @{tt}location@{utt} and @{tt}source@{utt}, and
@{tt}message@{utt} "Failed to fetch weather data from met.no". The usual
@{tt}error@{utt} texts:

@{bullet}"Could not find coordinates for location: Xyz" -- the place was
not found (met.no).
@{bullet}"Could not find coordinates for US location: Seattle" --
weather.gov found no US address.
@{bullet}The HTTP error of a service, for example "Client error '404 Not
Found' for url ...", or a network error. A service that does not connect
or answer within 30 seconds fails the call.

The plugin looks for a stopped run only before the lookup begins.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"location": "Munich"}
{"location": "Oslo", "days": 7}
{"location": "Oslo", "days": 2, "summary_format": "hourly"}
{"location": "London", "source": "wttr.in", "units": "imperial"}
{"location": "1600 Pennsylvania Ave NW, Washington, DC", "source": "weather.gov"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node sources "The sources"
@{h1}The sources

@{table}
source | Where | Location | Days | Units
met.no | worldwide | a place name, an address or "lat,lon" | 1 to 7 | always metric, wind in m/s
wttr.in | worldwide | a place name or "lat,lon" | 1 to 3 | metric or imperial
weather.gov | US only | a US street address with city and state | 1 to 7 | always imperial
@{body}

met.no finds the place with OpenStreetMap's
Nominatim and take its first hit. Nominatim allows about one request per
second; many lookups in a row can be refused.

@{h2}met.no

@{bullet}@{tt}current@{utt}: time, temperature, humidity, pressure,
wind speed (m/s) and wind direction (degrees) of the first time step.
@{bullet}@{tt}forecast@{utt}: one entry per local day with
@{tt}date@{utt}, @{tt}min_temp@{utt}, @{tt}max_temp@{utt},
@{tt}precipitation@{utt} (mm), @{tt}wind_max@{utt} (m/s) and
@{tt}symbols@{utt}, met.no's weather symbols ("rain", "partlycloudy_day").
@{bullet}Precipitation and symbols come from the six-hour periods that
start at 00, 06, 12 and 18 UTC: the total of their amounts and their
symbols in order. Before the first of these periods -- the rest of the
current one -- the rain is added hour by hour, so today's total covers
the rest of today.
@{bullet}min_temp and max_temp take every time step of the day and the
lowest and highest temperature met.no gives for each six-hour period, so
the later days, which have only four time steps, still get their real
range. wind_max is the highest wind of the time steps.
@{bullet}A six-hour period counts for the local day its middle falls
in. Where the local offset is not a multiple of six hours, up to three
hours of its rain and its temperatures belong to the neighbouring day.
@{bullet}The local day is estimated from the longitude -- one hour per 15
degrees, without daylight saving time -- so a day can start an hour off.
Today counts as the first day and begins at the current hour: its range,
rain and symbols cover only the rest of it.
@{bullet}With @{tt}summary_format: hourly@{utt} each day also has
@{tt}hourly@{utt}, its time steps: hourly for about two and a half days,
six-hourly after that, each with temperature, humidity, pressure, wind
and, where met.no gives it, @{tt}precipitation@{utt} (mm in the next six
hours) and a @{tt}weather_symbol@{utt}.

@{h2}wttr.in

@{bullet}@{tt}current@{utt}: temperature, feels_like, humidity,
wind speed and direction, pressure, visibility, a description and the
observation time. The numbers come as text ("15").
@{bullet}@{tt}forecast@{utt}: per day max_temp, min_temp and avg_temp
(as text), and from the day's three-hour steps @{tt}wind_speed@{utt} (the
highest), @{tt}humidity@{utt} (the mean, %), @{tt}chance_of_rain@{utt}
(the highest, %) and @{tt}weather_desc@{utt}, the different descriptions
in order ("Sunny", "Light rain").
@{bullet}With @{tt}summary_format: hourly@{utt} each day also has
@{tt}hours@{utt}, eight three-hour steps with temperature, wind,
humidity, pressure, description and chance_of_rain.
@{bullet}@{tt}units: imperial@{utt} gives °F and mph; any other value
metric (°C, km/h).

@{h2}weather.gov

The address goes to the US Census geocoder, which wants a street
address: "Seattle, WA" alone is not found. The forecast is the National
Weather Service's, in day and night periods.

@{bullet}No @{tt}current@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}forecast@{utt}: per date a @{tt}day@{utt} and a
@{tt}night@{utt} period, each with temperature, temperature_unit (F),
wind_speed and wind_direction as text ("5 to 10 mph"), a short and a
detailed forecast.
@{bullet}A night on its own is dated by the evening it belongs to (its
start minus 12 hours). A forecast issued in the evening starts with that
night ("Tonight"), one issued after midnight with the rest of the night
before ("Overnight", dated yesterday). The last date may have only its
day. At most twice @{tt}days@{utt} periods are read, so the list can
have one date more than asked for.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tool

The description and the parameters come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}, the same for every instance but the name:

@{code text}
Get current weather and multi-day forecasts. weather.gov covers only the US.
@{body}

The parameter texts are short: location "City or coordinates ('Munich',
'48.1351,11.5820'); weather.gov: US street address", units "Units; only
wttr.in follows them", summary_format "hourly adds the time
steps"; source has only its list of values. The schema's defaults
(met.no, 3, metric, daily) are the ones the code applies.

@{h2}Errors, word for word

@{bullet}"Missing required parameter: location"
@{bullet}"days must be a whole number from 1 to 7, got 'many'"
@{bullet}"Unsupported weather source: foo"
@{bullet}"Weather request cancelled by user"
@{bullet}"Could not find coordinates for location: Xyz"
@{bullet}"Could not find coordinates for US location: Seattle"

@{h2}The status line

"Fetching weather for Munich", then "Successfully fetched weather for
Munich", or "Exception during weather fetch: <error>" when the lookup
failed. A refused call shows its error text.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The tool definition is the same on every request, so the prompt cache
holds; the plugin adds nothing to the system prompt. The answer is an
ordinary tool result, appended. Measured for Munich: met.no about 600
characters for one day, 1,000 for three and 1,700 for seven; wttr.in
about 1,100 for three days; weather.gov 2,000 to
3,000. @{tt}summary_format: hourly@{utt} makes it many times larger --
met.no 17,000 characters for seven days, wttr.in 5,200 for three -- so
ask for it only when the course of a day matters.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}No sea data: the former source marine.weather.gov computed sea
temperature and wave height from latitude and month -- invented, so it
was removed. A source name it knew now answers an error.
@{bullet}Only wttr.in follows @{tt}units@{utt}; the answer's
@{tt}units@{utt} field says what the numbers are.
@{bullet}met.no's local day is estimated from the longitude and ignores
daylight saving time.
@{bullet}A stopped run is only noticed before the lookup begins.
@{bullet}Nothing is cached: every call asks the services again, and
Nominatim may refuse fast repeats.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration
(@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}):

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    weather:
      type: weather
      enabled: true
@{body}

An agent sees the tool when its allowlist names it:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+weather/*"
@{body}

It needs internet access to the services (api.met.no, wttr.in,
nominatim.openstreetmap.org, geocoding.geo.census.gov, api.weather.gov)
and httpx, which the core already installs. No key, no account.

@{h2}Server settings

There are none. The @{tt}ssl_verify@{utt} in the @{tt}config:@{utt} block
of @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt} is not read: certificates are always verified.

The plugin keeps nothing on disk and caches nothing; every call asks the
services again.
@endnode
